    extract (page read): {"metadata": {"description": "Table of contents", "MobileOptimized": "width", "HandheldFriendly": "true", "viewport": "width=device-width, initial-scale=1, shrink-to-fit=no", "title": "Reasonable care | Tax Practitioners Board", "og:image": "/themes/custom/custom/tpb_bario_sass/images/share_card_logo.png", "Generator": "Drupal 11 (http://drupal.org) + GovCMS (http://govcms.gov.au)", "language": "en", "ogImage": "/themes/custom/custom/tpb_bario_sass/images/share_card_logo.png", "favicon": "https://www.tpb.gov.au/themes/custom/custom/tpb_bario_sass/favicon.ico", "scrapeId": "01a0e5c5-c760-708a-8bb0-254a5f6f325c", "sourceURL": "https://www.tpb.gov.au/reasonable-care", "url": "https://www.tpb.gov.au/reasonable-care", "statusCode": 200, "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8", "proxyUsed": "basic", "cacheState": "hit", "cachedAt": "2026-09-28T02:08:28.580Z", "creditsUsed": 5, "concurrencyLimited": false}, "answer": "Code item 10 requires tax agents to take reasonable care in ensuring taxation laws are applied correctly to a client's circumstances when providing advice.

If a mistake is made in applying tax laws, it doesn't automatically mean a breach has occurred. However, if the mistake results from a lack of reasonable care, it can be considered a breach of the Code.

When a tax position is uncertain, reasonable care may involve consulting legislation, ATO materials, professional associations, or specialists.

The concept of a "reasonably arguable position" is not explicitly detailed in this section of the document. The document focuses on the requirement to apply taxation laws correctly and the care needed in doing so."}

